      content:
          '**CTCAE (Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events) Grading for Peripheral Neuropathy**:\n- **Grade 1**: Asymptomatic; loss of deep tendon reflexes or paresthesia only\n- **Grade 2**: Moderate symptoms; limiting instrumental ADLs (this patient\'s difficulty with buttons, keyboard use, and gait unsteadiness fits here)\n- **Grade 3**: Severe symptoms; limiting self-care ADLs; assistive device indicated\n- **Grade 4**: Life-threatening consequences; urgent intervention indicated\n\n**This patient is CTCAE Grade 2**, approaching Grade 3 given the fall risk and progressive trajectory.\n\n**Expected Electrodiagnostic (EDX) Findings**:\n- **Nerve conduction studies (NCS)**: Reduced or absent **sensory nerve action potential (SNAP) amplitudes** in sural, superficial peroneal, and median/ulnar sensory nerves bilaterally. Sensory conduction velocities may be mildly reduced. Motor conduction studies are typically **normal or near-normal** (taxane neuropathy is predominantly sensory axonal). Compound muscle action potential (CMAP) amplitudes are usually preserved.\n- **EMG**: Typically **normal** needle EMG unless severe. May show mild denervation (fibrillations, positive sharp waves) in distal intrinsic foot muscles in advanced cases.\n- **Pattern**: Length-dependent, symmetric, sensory-predominant **axonal** neuropathy.\n\n**Key Differential Diagnoses**:\n1. **Diabetic neuropathy** — check HbA1c, fasting glucose\n2. **B12 deficiency** — especially with concurrent metformin use or poor nutrition during chemo\n3. **Paraneoplastic neuropathy** — anti-Hu antibodies; would expect more asymmetric or rapidly progressive course\n4. **Compression neuropathy** — carpal tunnel or tarsal tunnel syndrome superimposed\n5. **Hypothyroidism** — check TSH\n6. **Alcohol-related neuropathy** — obtain alcohol use history\n\n**Recommended workup**: Vitamin B12, methylmalonic acid, HbA1c, TSH, serum protein electrophoresis, and consider EDX to document baseline severity and guide dose modifications.',
