      content:
          '**Cancer Survivorship Rehabilitation Model**:\n\n1. **Survivorship Care Plan**: Per ASCO/ACS guidelines, a formal survivorship care plan should be created documenting her treatment history, known side effects (CIPN), surveillance schedule, and rehabilitation needs. This is shared with the patient, oncologist, and primary care physician.\n\n2. **Cancer Rehabilitation Continuum**:\n- **Preventive (prehabilitation)**: Ideally, baseline functional assessment and exercise counseling would have occurred before chemotherapy started.\n- **Restorative**: Current phase — active therapy to restore function lost to CIPN.\n- **Supportive**: Ongoing management of persistent impairments.\n- **Palliative**: Not applicable here but part of the continuum.\n\n3. **Prospective Surveillance Model (PSM)**: Standardized screening for impairments at regular intervals (baseline, mid-treatment, end of treatment, 6 months, 12 months). Uses validated tools such as the FACT-Taxane subscale, CIPN-20 questionnaire, or Brief Pain Inventory.\n\n4. **Multidisciplinary Coordination**:\n- **Oncology**: Ongoing tamoxifen management, cancer surveillance, communication about CIPN trajectory.\n- **PM&R/Cancer Rehab**: Quarterly functional assessments, therapy prescriptions, pharmacologic management of CIPN.\n- **PT/OT**: Goal-directed therapy programs with clear discharge criteria and home exercise program.\n- **Psychology**: Screen for cancer-related distress, anxiety, fear of recurrence.\n- **Primary Care**: Long-term cardiovascular monitoring (anthracycline exposure), bone health (tamoxifen effects).\n\n5. **Insurance and Access**: Cancer rehabilitation services are covered under standard therapy benefits. Advocate for therapy referrals with specific cancer rehabilitation diagnoses (G62.0 — drug-induced polyneuropathy) to ensure appropriate authorization.',
